{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-45b0286685414e4790bb260a939fe6e7","ordinal":121,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.121","text":"\n\nSince, then, everything that appears is observed in some place, from some distance and in some position, and each of these produces great variation in the impressions, as we have pointed out, we will be forced by this mode too to arrive at suspension of judgment. For anyone who wants to prefer some of these impressions to others will be attempting the impossible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a8f609307ef43798e08ae4223530246","ordinal":122,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.122","text":"\n\nIf he makes his pronouncement simply and without proof, he will not be credible. If he wants to use a proof, then if he says the proof is false, he will refute himself; and if he says the proof is true, he will be asked for a proof that it is true, and for another proof of that one (since it too must be true), and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to produce infinitely many proofs;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9186c326c2de4f47800a773bdb01e53b","ordinal":123,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.123","text":"\n\nso he will not be able to prefer one impression to another with a proof either. And if no one can decide between the impressions just mentioned either without proof or with proof, suspension of judgment follows. We can perhaps say what each thing appears like in this position, or at this distance, or in this place; but for the reasons given we cannot declare what it is like in its nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b4002473b7ae498290379af4c1c1601b","ordinal":124,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.124","text":"\n\nThe sixth mode is the one depending on admixtures. By it we conclude that, since none of the underlying objects strikes us by itself but always together with something, we can perhaps say what the mixture of the external object and the thing it is observed together with is like, but we could not say what the external object is like in its pure state. That none of the external objects strikes us by itself, but always together with something, and that it is observed as different because of this, is evident, I think."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-95ce6fc2b12642b2ab2f3ef247b5189f","ordinal":125,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.125","text":"\n\nOur complexion, at any rate, is seen as one color in warm air and another in cold, and we could not say what our complexion is like by nature, only what it is like as observed together with each of these. The same sound appears one way in thin air and another in dense air. Perfumes are more pungent in the bathhouse and in the sun than in chilly air. And a body surrounded by water is light, but surrounded by air it is heavy."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cdd8b8fa7d33463c9a948c594b43014e","ordinal":126,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.126","text":"\n\nTo set aside admixture from outside as well: our eyes contain within themselves both membranes and fluids. So, since visible things are not observed without these, they will not be apprehended accurately; for what we perceive is the mixture. That is why people with jaundice see everything yellow, and those with bloodshot eyes see everything reddish. And since the same sound appears one way in open places and another in narrow, winding ones, one way in clear air and another in murky air, it is likely that we do not perceive sound in its pure state either. For the ears have winding, narrow passages, and they are muddied by vaporous effluvia, which are said to be carried up from the regions around the head."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b3635212930741d79e7932363db1eba0","ordinal":127,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.127","text":"\n\nAgain, since there are substances present in the nostrils and in the organs of taste, we perceive tastes and smells together with them, and not in a pure state. So, because of admixtures, the senses do not perceive what external objects are accurately like."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-11f76b890b3d497da8b3ca33d7df9588","ordinal":128,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.128","text":"\n\nNor does the intellect—chiefly because the senses, which are its guides, go wrong; but perhaps it too adds some admixture of its own to what the senses report. For in each of the places where the dogmatists think the ruling part is located, we observe certain humors present—whether one wants to place it in the brain, or in the heart, or in any other part of the animal whatever. So by this mode too we see that, having nothing to say about the nature of external objects, we are forced to suspend judgment.\n〔1〕 The “ruling part” (hēgemonikon) is the Stoic term for the soul’s command center, which some placed in the heart and others in the brain."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-628f5acb3a1c42b5830ab2a8c6ba81e1","ordinal":129,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.129","text":"\n\nThe seventh mode, we said, is the one depending on the quantities and preparations of underlying objects—by “preparations” we mean compositions in general. That by this mode too we are forced to suspend judgment about the nature of things is clear. Shavings of goat’s horn, for example, appear white when observed simply, without being put together; but put together in the actual horn they are seen as black. And silver filings appear black on their own, but together with the whole mass they strike us as white."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f75e79bdcb3e430bb1ddd27d6ddb7291","ordinal":130,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.130","text":"\n\nPieces of Taenarian marble look white when they are polished, but together with the whole block they appear yellow. Grains of sand scattered apart appear rough, but put together in a heap they affect our senses softly. And hellebore taken fine and powdery causes choking, but not when it is coarse-ground."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-12f9a01870c44f879eaf2c8d45b6d797","ordinal":131,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.131","text":"\n\nWine drunk in moderation strengthens us, but taken in larger quantity it enfeebles the body. Food likewise shows different effects depending on quantity: often, at any rate, when too much is eaten it purges the body through indigestion and bilious attacks."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-83c7a9a87e8e468a80846061a0e14a90","ordinal":132,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.132","text":"\n\nHere too, then, we will be able to say what a fine shaving of horn is like and what a horn composed of many fine shavings is like; what silver in small particles is like and what silver composed of many is like; what a tiny piece of Taenarian marble is like and what the stone composed of many small pieces is like; and, in the case of sand, hellebore, wine and food, what they are like relatively. But we will not be able to say what the nature of things is in itself, because of the variation among impressions that depends on compositions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aac6ab8a09934f0f819b965b12723170","ordinal":133,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.133","text":"\n\nIn general, it seems, even beneficial things become harmful when they are used in immoderate quantities, and things that seem harmful do no harm when taken in extremely small amounts. The strongest evidence for this argument is what we observe in the effects of medicines. There, an exact mixture of the simple drugs makes the compound beneficial; but sometimes, when the slightest weight is overlooked, it is not only not beneficial but extremely harmful, and often poisonous."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-40298f8ff2cb431086200da57328fd9c","ordinal":134,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.134","text":"\n\nSo the argument from quantities and preparations throws the reality of external objects into confusion. That is why this mode too would reasonably bring us round to suspension of judgment, since we cannot make any pure pronouncement about the nature of external objects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ae7835ff4dd64688ac7d1b1213f1984f","ordinal":135,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.135","text":"\n\nThe eighth mode is the one from relativity, by which we conclude that, since everything is relative, we will suspend judgment about what things are absolutely and in their nature. But we must realize that here, as elsewhere, we use “is” loosely in place of “appears,” saying in effect “everything appears relative.” This is said in two ways: first, relative to what judges (for the external object being judged appears relative to what judges it); and second, relative to the things observed along with it, as right is relative to left."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f9b81d3abd5c4a1daeb76dda43fbe847","ordinal":136,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.136","text":"\n\nThat everything is relative we have already reasoned earlier: with respect to what judges, in that each thing appears relative to this animal, this human being and this sense, and relative to this kind of circumstance; and with respect to the things observed along with it, in that each thing appears relative to this admixture, this manner, this composition and quantity, and this position."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-310826c681d94d29831442ff9b7b082f","ordinal":137,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.137","text":"\n\nIt is also possible to conclude in a separate way that everything is relative, as follows. Do things that are “by difference” differ from relatives or not? If they do not differ, then they too are relative. If they do differ, then, since everything that differs is relative (for it is spoken of relative to what it differs from), things that are by difference are relative.\n〔1〕 Things “by difference” is a technical term for things with an intrinsic, non-relational character, as opposed to relatives."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a09db0fc29145dca02e8ea998a02450","ordinal":138,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.138","text":"\n\nAgain, of the things that exist, according to the dogmatists, some are highest genera, some lowest species, and some both genera and species; but all of these are relative; therefore everything is relative. Further, of the things that exist some are evident and some non-evident, as they themselves say; the things that appear are signifiers, and the non-evident things are signified by the things that appear—for according to them the things that appear are a glimpse of the non-evident. But signifier and signified are relative; therefore everything is relative."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a1b71cbc87ec4f94ac60761dc7874d74","ordinal":139,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.139","text":"\n\nIn addition, of the things that exist some are similar and some dissimilar, some equal and some unequal; but these are relative; therefore everything is relative. And whoever says that not everything is relative confirms that everything is relative. For by the very arguments with which he opposes us, he shows that “everything is relative” is itself relative to us, and not universal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-84264742db0542c59a179fdb94750744","ordinal":140,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.140","text":"\n\nAt any rate, once we have established in this way that everything is relative, it is clear from then on that we will not be able to say what each of the underlying objects is like in its own nature and in its pure state, but only what it appears like in relation to something. ⟨From this⟩ it follows that we must suspend judgment about the nature of things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-018ce9dd005f44b79f8eb23206811030","ordinal":141,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.141","text":"\n\nAbout the mode depending on constant or rare encounters, which we said was ninth in order, we set out something like the following. The sun is surely far more astonishing than a comet. But since we see the sun constantly and a comet rarely, we are so astonished at the comet that we even take it for a divine portent, while we are not astonished at the sun at all. Yet if we imagine the sun appearing rarely and setting rarely, lighting up everything all at once and suddenly plunging everything into shadow, we will see much to be astonished at in the matter."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a8da24d728bd478b81ed1d1c30664e13","ordinal":142,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.142","text":"\n\nAn earthquake, too, does not alarm those who experience it for the first time and those who have grown used to it in the same way. How astonished a person is by the sea when seeing it for the first time! Again, the beauty of a human body, seen suddenly and for the first time, moves us more than if seeing it became a habit. And rare things seem precious, while things that are familiar to us and easy to come by do not seem so at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-506a7d747bc340178b7869d346a22d10","ordinal":143,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.143","text":"\n\nAt any rate, if we imagine water being scarce, how much more precious it would appear to us than everything that seems precious! Or if we picture gold simply strewn about on the ground in large amounts, like stones, to whom do we think it will be precious, or worth locking up as it is now?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-31f21164a03d4b87affca827ed90f821","ordinal":144,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.144","text":"\n\nSince, then, the same things seem at one time astonishing or precious and at another time not, depending on whether they are encountered constantly or rarely, we conclude that we will perhaps be able to say what each of these appears like when encountered constantly or rarely, but we cannot state what each external object is like in itself. So by this mode too we suspend judgment about them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a513b66f10974ed28132b19ce3b0858e","ordinal":145,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.145","text":"\n\nThe tenth mode, which bears most of all on ethics, is the one depending on ways of life, customs, laws, beliefs in myth and dogmatic suppositions. A way of life is a choice of a life, or of some course of conduct, adopted by one person or by many—for example, by Diogenes or by the Spartans.\n〔1〕 Diogenes of Sinope (fourth century BC), the founding figure of the Cynics, was famous for living in poverty and flouting convention."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-06f1aa3f733d4536a6e7f50b991d95a5","ordinal":146,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.146","text":"\n\nA law is a written contract among citizens, and whoever transgresses it is punished. A custom or habit (there is no difference) is the common acceptance of some practice by many people, and whoever transgresses it is not necessarily punished. For example, it is a law not to commit adultery, and a custom among us not to have sex with a woman in public."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-785d033c7fba402c8678d1dbf9029e61","ordinal":147,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.147","text":"\n\nA mythical belief is the acceptance of things that never happened and were made up, such as, among others, the stories told about Cronus; for these lead many people to believe them. A dogmatic supposition is the acceptance of something that seems to be established by reasoning by analogy or by some proof—for example, that the elements of existing things are atoms, or homoeomeries, ⟨or⟩ minimal parts, or something else.\n〔1〕 Atoms were the elements of Democritus and Epicurus; “homoeomeries” (stuffs whose every part is like the whole) those of Anaxagoras; “minimal parts” those of Diodorus Cronus."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e729b6e528094aba9f30d81a36e90f25","ordinal":148,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.148","text":"\n\nWe oppose each of these sometimes to itself and sometimes to each of the others. Custom to custom, for example, like this: some Ethiopians tattoo their babies, while we do not; the Persians think it seemly to wear brightly dyed, ankle-length clothing, while we think it unseemly; and the Indians have sex with their women in public, while most other peoples consider this shameful."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-661ae917762746c890199d0cd1b4aab4","ordinal":149,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.149","text":"\n\nLaw to law, like this: among the Romans, a man who renounces his father’s estate does not pay his father’s debts, but among the Rhodians he pays them in any case; and among the Tauri in Scythia it was the law that strangers be sacrificed to Artemis, while among us it is forbidden for a human being to be killed at a sacred place.\n〔1〕 The Tauri lived in the Crimea; their sacrifice of strangers is the background of Euripides’ Iphigenia among the Taurians."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-448834704dc54dedb9a0ae7ac60edcf2","ordinal":150,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.150","text":"\n\nWay of life to way of life, when we oppose the way of life of Diogenes to that of Aristippus, or that of the Spartans to that of the Italians. Mythical belief to mythical belief, when ⟨we say⟩ that in one place Zeus is said in myth to be “father of men and gods,” and in another Ocean, as when people say “Ocean, origin of the gods, and mother Tethys.” We oppose dogmatic suppositions to one another\n〔1〕 Aristippus of Cyrene, a follower of Socrates, was known for a life of comfort and pleasure—the opposite of Diogenes’ austerity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dcdc324a76b24227929f53cd4326c636","ordinal":151,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.151","text":"\n\nwhen we say that some declare there is one element and others infinitely many; that some declare the soul mortal and others immortal; and that some say our affairs are governed by the providence of the gods, and others that they are governed without providence."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-40931fbcc9344cedbc641e0ca12ebf52","ordinal":152,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.152","text":"\n\nWe oppose custom to the others: to law, for example, when we say that among the Persians it is the custom to engage in sex between men, while among the Romans it is forbidden by law to do so; that among us adultery is forbidden, while among the Massagetae it is handed down by custom as a matter of indifference, as Eudoxus of Cnidus reports in the first book of his Circuit of the Earth; and that among us it is forbidden to have sex with one’s mother, while among the Persians it is very much the custom to marry in this way. The Egyptians, too, marry their sisters, which among us is forbidden by law.\n〔1〕 The Massagetae were nomads east of the Caspian Sea; Eudoxus of Cnidus was a fourth-century BC mathematician and geographer."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a074f9a26fdd4376ba9a231e4a416285","ordinal":153,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.153","text":"\n\nCustom is opposed to way of life when most people withdraw in private to have sex with their wives, while Crates did so with Hipparchia in public; and when Diogenes went about in a tunic that left one shoulder bare, while we dress as we are accustomed to.\n〔1〕 Crates of Thebes, a pupil of Diogenes, and his wife Hipparchia were both Cynic philosophers."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-027e4742194a46f4aaff1a63d29c3a6a","ordinal":154,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.154","text":"\n\nTo mythical belief, when the myths say that Cronus ate his own children, while it is our custom to look after our children; and when among us it is the habit to revere the gods as good and untouched by evils, while the poets portray them being wounded and envying one another."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a81fff7bfdb84278bfe16e04638e59a0","ordinal":155,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.155","text":"\n\nTo dogmatic supposition, when it is our custom to ask the gods for good things, while Epicurus says that the divine pays no attention to us; and when Aristippus considers it a matter of indifference to wear women’s clothing, while we consider it shameful."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-615ef882f6a04f74a2a249a3ffb095aa","ordinal":156,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.156","text":"\n\nWe oppose way of life to law when, though there is a law against striking a free and well-born man, pancratiasts strike one another because of the way of life they lead; and when, though killing a person is forbidden, gladiators kill one another for the same reason.\n〔1〕 The pancration was a Greek combat sport combining boxing and wrestling."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6eac0b713164450ab5072bfb83ef2e71","ordinal":157,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.157","text":"\n\nWe oppose mythical belief to way of life when we say that the myths tell of Heracles at Omphale’s house carding wool, putting up with slavery, and doing things that no one with even moderately high principles would have done, while Heracles’ way of life was noble.\n〔1〕 Omphale was a queen of Lydia to whom Heracles was sold as a slave."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b04ef65cca3f46cdad7c4db4f75095ea","ordinal":158,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.158","text":"\n\nTo dogmatic supposition, when athletes, striving for glory as ⟨something⟩ good, take up a toilsome way of life for its sake, while many philosophers hold the belief that glory is worthless."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bfb99e9664be4767b7572859c365f6fc","ordinal":159,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.159","text":"\n\nWe oppose law to mythical belief when the poets portray the gods committing adultery and engaging in sex between males, while the law among us forbids doing these things; and to dogmatic"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4486430aef86405cbcc5e42eed95de7d","ordinal":160,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.160","text":"\n\nsupposition, when Chrysippus and his followers say that having sex with one’s mother or sister is a matter of indifference, while the law forbids these things."}]}